      "question": "What are some causes of today’s downturn in AI stocks? Explain the main connections clearly.",
      "budget": {
        "max_tool_calls": 8,
        "run_timeout_seconds": 300.0,
        "llm_step_timeout_seconds": 90,
        "prefetch_timeout_seconds": 15,
        "tool_call_timeout_seconds": 45
      },
      "checkpoint_saved": true,
      "source_policy": "retained_only",
      "write_policy": "none",
      "tool_calls": [
        {
          "name": "research.search_evidence",
          "status": "completed",
          "carried_forward": false
        },
        {
          "name": "research.search_evidence",
          "status": "completed",
          "carried_forward": false
        },
        {
          "name": "research.search_evidence",
          "status": "completed",
          "carried_forward": false
        }
      ],
      "public_paragraph_count": 5,
      "public_citation_count": 24,
      "private_checkpoint_exposed": false,
      "generated_searchable_chunks": 0,
      "answer_markdown": "AI stocks fell on 8 October 2026, but the move was not a crash and no single cause is confirmed. Two forces overlapped inside a broadly weaker market, and each force pushed the same group of shares down for a different reason.\n\nThe first force was weaker expected demand. The Financial Times reported that OpenAI's yearly revenue run rate is near $50 billion, about $20 billion below what earlier signals suggested (\"'OpenAI annualised revenues $20bn less than previously signalled' - Financial Times\"). AI chip and data-center spending depends on customers like OpenAI, so a lower revenue path means less future spending on computing. Oracle fell on that report, and Nvidia fell with it (\"Oracle Stock Drops Thursday: What's Happening?\", \"OpenAI Revenue $20B Below Previous Reports, ORCL, NVDA Tumble\").\n\nThe second force was a higher discount rate. The 10-year Treasury yield — the interest rate the government pays to borrow for 10 years, used as a benchmark for other investments — came close to 5.35% (\"Stock Market Today: S&P 500 Slips as Oil Spikes 5%, 10-Year Yields Near 5.35%\"). Higher yields lower the present value of profits that arrive years from now and raise borrowing costs for capital-heavy data centers. That explains why Bloom Energy fell on the yield move (\"Why Is Bloom Energy Stock Falling on Thursday?\") and why \"neoclouds\" — companies that rent out AI computing capacity — came under pressure (\"Bitcoin Slide Meets 5.7% Bond Yields: Neoclouds Get Hit From Both Sides\"). Falling crypto prices added to this: bitcoin dropped below $81,000 with about $974 million of crypto liquidations, which dragged down crypto-linked AI infrastructure shares (\"TradingView: Bitcoin Drops Below $81K, Triggering Nearly $1B Crypto Liquidation Wave\").\n\nA third cluster is about money for the buildout. A Nvidia-backed initial public offering (a company's first sale of shares to the public) drew very weak demand, Nvidia-backed Firmus postponed its IPO, SpaceX plans about $40 billion of debt to buy Nvidia chips with record credit-default swaps (insurance-like contracts that signal lenders see more risk), and Broadcom's AI financing deals drew analyst debt warnings (\"'Nvidia-Backed IPO's Cratering Demand Sends Warning on AI Funding' - Bloomberg\", \"'Nvidia-Backed Firmus Is Set to Postpone IPO, Weigh Private Round'\", \"Elon Musk Is Betting $40 Billion on Nvidia Chips — SpaceX Investors Aren't So Sure\", \"What's Going On With Broadcom Stock Thursday?\"). If buildout money becomes more expensive or harder to raise, spending can slow. Oil rose 5% with Brent above $105 after Gulf tanker attacks, which fed inflation worries and pushed yields higher, and Ray Dalio's bubble warning added to the mood (\"Stock Market Today\", \"AI Bubble Nearing a Breaking Point? Ray Dalio's Warning\"). AI hardware fell too: Astera Labs -9.2%, TTM Technologies -8.2%, and AXT -10% (\"Astera Labs (NASDAQ:ALAB) Stock Price Down 9.2%\", \"TTM Technologies (NASDAQ:TTMI) Shares Down 8.2%\", \"AXT slumps 10%\").\n\nTreat the attribution with care: most sources are same-day reports and do not confirm causes. Counterweights also exist, including Nvidia's $250 billion OpenAI financing backstop, TSMC's planned capital spending of up to $64 billion, and US AI research pledges (\"CRWV, NBIS Stocks Climb – Nvidia's $250B OpenAI Guarantee Is A 'Constructive' Signal\", \"EXCLUSIVE: TSMC Plans Up to $64 Billion in Capex\")."
    },
