      "public_paragraph_count": 2,
      "public_citation_count": 24,
      "private_checkpoint_exposed": false,
      "generated_searchable_chunks": 0,
      "answer_markdown": "The weaker demand signal matters most: the Financial Times reported that OpenAI's yearly revenue run rate is near $50 billion, about $20 billion below earlier signals, and because AI chip and data-center spending depends on buyers like OpenAI, Oracle and Nvidia fell with it ([\"'OpenAI annualised revenues $20bn less than previously signalled' - Financial Times\"](https://benzinga.com/news/26/10/62253618/openai-annualised-revenues-20bn-less-than-previously-signalled-financial-times), [\"Oracle Stock Drops Thursday: What's Happening?\"](https://benzinga.com/trading-ideas/movers/26/10/62258380/oracle-stock-drops-thursday-whats-happening)).\n\nThe 10-year Treasury yield near 5.35% and a 5% jump in oil prices pushed the whole market down and made the AI drop deeper, but they hit every sector, so they added to the fall rather than started it ([\"Stock Market Today: S&P 500 Slips as Oil Spikes 5%, 10-Year Yields Near 5.35%\"](https://benzinga.com/markets/equities/26/10/62255036/stock-market-today-sp-500-slips-oil-rallies-yields))."
    }
